isted, that blocking it was forced, and that there are no additional threats for this position, and that blocking at K8 is better. I can\'t wait to see what Black does next!\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: The current board state presents an immediate lethal threat from Black. White\'s horizontal line of four stones on Row 9 (F9-I9) is blocked at both ends (E9 and J9) and is therefore ineffective. However, Black has established a diagonal "Open 3" (also known as a Live 3) with stones at J9, I10, and H11. This line extends to the empty squares K8 (top-right) and G12 (bottom-left). If White does not block this turn, Black will play on one of these squares to create an "Open 4" (Live 4), which ensures a win on the subsequent move. White has no immediate winning combinations (like 4-in-a-row or VCF) to ignore this threat, so a defensive move is forced.\n\nReason: White must block the diagonal line J9-I10-H11 at either K8 or G12. Blocking at K8 is the better choice for two reasons:\n1.  **Centrality:** K8 is closer to the center of the board (Row 8) compared to G12 (Row 12). Controlling the center and restricting the opponent\'s expansion towards the open K-column is generally strategically superior to playing towards the edge.\n2.  **Connectivity:** A White stone at K8 forms a remote connection (a "broken 2" with a gap at J8) with the White stone at I8. While not an immediate strong line, it offers more potential for future link-ups than a stone at G12, which would be more isolated.\n\nAnswer: { "col": "K", "row": 8 }'}
